0,Djadjawurrung - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Djadjawurrung,"Dja Dja Wurrung elder Aunty Sue Rankin at the Human Rights Day gathering in Melbourne, 2005 The Djadjawurrung or Dja Dja Wurrung, also known as the Djaara or Jajowrong people and Loddon River tribe, are an Aboriginal Australian people who are the traditional owners of lands including the water catchment areas of the Loddon and Avoca rivers in the Bendigo region of central Victoria, Australia ...",Djadjawurrung,Australian
1,Djadjawurrung language - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Djadjawurrung_language,"Djadjawurrung (also Jaara, Ngurai-illam-wurrung) is an Aboriginal Australian language spoken by the Dja Dja Wurrung people of the Kulin nation of central Victoria.",Djadjawurrung,Australian
2,Djadjawurrung — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Djadjawurrung,"The Dja Dja Wurrung, also known as Djadjawurrung or Jaara, are an Aboriginal Australian people and Traditional Owners of Country spanning central Victoria, including the catchments of the Loddon (Bulutjal Yaluk) and Avoca rivers and extending across shires such as Buloke, Loddon, Campaspe, Bendigo, Mount Alexander, Pyrenees, and Hepburn. [1] [2] Their language, Dja Dja Wurrung, belongs to the ...",Djadjawurrung,Australian
3,Djadjawurrung language — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/djadjawurrung_language,"The Djadjawurrung language, also known as Dja Dja Wurrung, is an Indigenous Australian language traditionally spoken by the Dja Dja Wurrung people (also known as Jaara), the Traditional Owners of Djandak (Country) spanning central Victoria from the Great Dividing Range to the Loddon River and Pyrenees Ranges, including areas around present-day towns such as Castlemaine, Bendigo, and St Arnaud ...",Djadjawurrung,Australian
